WHAT WAS TAKEN. Five glyphs, held in one module.

`src/data/mapIcons.js` holds the artwork:

    security_camera         — the surveillance-camera billboard, drawn by
                              `src/data/cctv.js`.

    fighter_jet             — `military=airfield`, drawn by
                              `src/data/militarySiteIcons.js`.

    cooling_tower_radiation — a NUCLEAR generating station, drawn by
                              `src/data/plantFiliereIcons.js` for the EDF fleet
                              layer (`src/data/edfPowerPlants.js`).

    power_tower             — a lattice transmission pylon, drawn by
                              `src/data/powerGrid.js` along the mapped overhead
                              routes.

    radiation               — the trefoil alone, drawn by
                              `src/data/medecinFamilyIcons.js` for the
                              « Imagerie et biologie » family of the French
                              conventioned-doctors layer.

Temaki is the icon set of the OpenStreetMap iD editor and covers subjects no
interface icon set does; a wall-mounted surveillance camera is one of them, a
combat aircraft is another, and so is a power station drawn by what it splits.
Material Symbols has none of the five: what `security_camera` replaced was this
project's own drawing, what `fighter_jet` replaced was Material's `flight` — an
airliner, which is the right glyph for the civil aerodrome pack next door and
the wrong one for an air base — Material has no nuclear glyph at all, and its
nearest thing to a pylon is `electric_bolt`, a lightning flash, which is a
symbol for ELECTRICITY rather than a picture of the structure carrying it.

WHY A PYLON IS DRAWN AT ALL. `power_tower` is the glyph iD puts on the tag, and
the tag is what OpenStreetMap has mapped: a transmission line's node list IS its
pylon list. Measured 2026-09-14 against the live proxy, 574 of 574 nodes tagged
`power=tower` around Bayonne and 750 of 775 around Saclay are vertices of an
overhead `power=line` way this layer draws — and every one of the 25 Saclay
misses is a pylon of a way it deliberately does NOT draw, one of them a
`disused:power=line` whose steel is still standing. The layer therefore draws
the glyph on the way's own mapped vertices, at the spacing the camera can read,
and never at a position it invented between them.

WHY THE BARE TREFOIL IS LEGITIMATE HERE, HAVING BEEN REJECTED NEXT DOOR.

`plantFiliereIcons.js` records a refusal to draw the bare trefoil for the EDF
generating fleet: "the bare trefoil is a HAZARD sign — a claim this map does not
make and has no data for". That refusal stands where it was made. Nothing in the
EDF file says a station emits anything, so the symbol there would have been an
assertion with nothing behind it.

For the doctors layer the data IS the assertion. The family the trefoil marks is
built from the register's own specialty codes, and 90.8 % of its 40 009 entries
are `06` Radiologue, `72` médecine nucléaire, `74` Cancérologue radiothérapeute
and `76` Radiothérapeute — practices that use ionising radiation by definition,
and that post this exact sign on their own doors under French radiation-protection
rules. It is read as the CARTOGRAPHIC symbol for those specialties, the same
reading `cooling_tower_radiation` already carries in this project.

The alternatives were measured on a contact sheet over a real Lyon capture at
30 / 24 / 19 / 15 / 12 px: Material's `science` (a flask) and `biotech` (a
microscope) both read cleanly at 12 px and both name the family's remaining
9.2 % — the pathologists and medical biologists — instead of its 90.8 %.
Material's `radiology` names the right specialty and is mush below 19 px.

WHY THE VARIANT WITH THE TREFOIL. Temaki publishes a plain `cooling_tower` as
well, and it is the wrong glyph here: coal plants have cooling towers too, and
this layer draws them a hundred kilometres apart on the same screen. The trefoil
is what makes the tower nuclear. It is read here as a CARTOGRAPHIC symbol — the
one OpenStreetMap renderers use for `plant:source=nuclear` — and not as a hazard
notice, which is a claim this project has no data for and does not make.

WHY THE CAMERA REPLACED THIS PROJECT'S OWN DRAWING. Not taste — a colour bug.

The old camera baked cyan into the artwork (`#75e7ff` strokes over dark fills)
while the layer tints the billboard to mark state: `#6be8ff` at rest, `#ffd97a`
for the ACTIVE camera. Cesium multiplies `billboard.color` into the texture, so
the selected camera rendered #75e7ff × #ffd97a = **#75c57a**, a green. White
artwork makes that multiply an identity and the active camera is amber, as the
layer always intended.

WHAT WAS CHANGED. No coordinate.

Only the `d` path strings are vendored, in Temaki's own space, NOT rescaled.
Temaki is not uniform about that space: `security_camera` and
`cooling_tower_radiation` are published in `0 0 15 15`, as is `power_tower`, and
`fighter_jet` in `0 0 48 48`. Each box is DECLARED (`MAP_ICON_BOX` in `src/data/mapIcons.js`)
rather than normalised, because rescaling is a redraw and a redraw is no longer
the artwork that was evaluated; everything downstream — the padded viewBox, the
halo width, the transform that fits a silhouette into a 96-unit plate — is
expressed as a ratio of that box.

The bare `radiation` trefoil is one path carrying four subpaths — three blades
and the hub — and is kept whole for the same reason as the rest.

Temaki publishes the camera as three sibling paths — a body, a wall bracket, and
a hood whose lens is a counter-wound subpath — and they are kept whole, in
order, and are never merged: merging them would fill the lens. The cooling tower
is one path carrying four counter-wound subpaths, the radiation trefoil, and is
kept whole for the same reason: merged, the tower has no trefoil on it and is no
longer a nuclear station — it is what a coal plant looks like. The PYLON is one
path too, carrying the three crossarm diamonds as counter-wound subpaths; merged
it is a black obelisk, which is what a chimney looks like.

THE TREFOIL IS READ HERE AS A CARTOGRAPHIC SYMBOL — on the cooling tower, the
one OpenStreetMap renderers use for `plant:source=nuclear`; on its own, the one
French radiology, nuclear-medicine and radiotherapy practices post on their own
doors. In neither case is it a hazard notice, which is a claim this project has
no measurement for and does not make.

At render time this project draws the artwork in one of two ways, neither of
which touches a coordinate. The CAMERA is drawn twice — once as a wide dark
stroke (a halo, so the glyph stays readable over pale terrain) and once as a
white fill (so Cesium's per-billboard colour multiply can tint it) — and its
viewBox is padded to `-1 -1 17 17` so that halo is not clipped, since the glyph
spans the full published box from x=0 to x=15. The PYLON is drawn the same way,
halo and white fill, so the layer's per-band voltage colour multiplies onto it
exactly and a 400 kV pylon reads as the colour of the 400 kV line it stands on.
The JET, the COOLING TOWER and the TREFOIL
are instead PUNCHED out of a tinted plate through an SVG `<mask>`: they mark
PLACES on a photograph of places, where a bare silhouette is unfindable below
18 px. The path geometry itself is untouched in all three, and
`src/data/mapIcons.test.mjs` pins it.
